WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.017 --> 00:00:11.486
A small desktop: three windows,
a dock, twelve photographs.

2
00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:14.884
Drag a window. It glides, and settles.

3
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.438
Every move is worked out
by one neural network,

4
00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:23.315
built with a library called
Handwired. This window shows it, live.

5
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:29.562
Even the wallpaper maps it: one arc for
each weight, each number it multiplies by.

6
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.976
Nine thousand, nine hundred and nine.

7
00:00:34.142 --> 00:00:36.184
Where did those weights come from?

8
00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:42.175
Somebody trained it. A tool converted
ordinary code. Or it isn’t really a network.

9
00:00:42.725 --> 00:00:47.705
They hang on this copper line, as
paper tags. We'll test every one.

10
00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:52.833
And one more tag, blank for now:
why would anyone build it this way?

11
00:00:54.308 --> 00:01:01.517
A scrap of JavaScript a child could check.
If a is more than four, ten. If not, twelve.

12
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:07.992
Build it from ramps, the stuff of neural
networks. Five gives ten; three, twelve.

13
00:01:08.542 --> 00:01:10.527
Give it four and a half.

14
00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:13.976
Eleven. Neither ten nor twelve.

15
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:21.028
Nothing’s broken. It was built for whole
numbers, and it took that on trust.

16
00:01:21.578 --> 00:01:25.595
This film is about promises
like that: who tied them on,

17
00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:28.206
who doubted them, and how long they held.

18
00:01:43.392 --> 00:01:47.444
In 1943, Warren McCulloch and
Walter Pitts tied on the oldest tag.

19
00:01:47.444 --> 00:01:50.357
Handwired never cites them.

20
00:01:50.907 --> 00:01:54.564
The tag: a neuron is a logic gate.

21
00:01:54.564 --> 00:01:59.137
Neural events, they wrote, "can be
treated by means of propositional logic."

22
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.382
Nothing in their nets learned. Someone
chose every connection, by hand.

23
00:02:07.142 --> 00:02:13.747
In 1951, at RAND, Stephen Kleene
showed such a net is a finite machine.

24
00:02:14.097 --> 00:02:17.591
He called the patterns it
can recognise regular events.

25
00:02:18.041 --> 00:02:22.674
Our reading: a finite net
remembers only so much;

26
00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:27.584
anything unbounded comes from
outside. Hang that on the line.

27
00:02:29.058 --> 00:02:33.981
In 2020, one human neuron's branches
answered either input but not both:

28
00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:37.718
a job thought to need layers of gates.

29
00:02:38.168 --> 00:02:42.904
As biology, the logic-gate tag frayed.
As engineering, it never came down.

30
00:02:44.392 --> 00:02:48.594
In 2019, a younger tag: learn
the weights, don’t write them.

31
00:02:48.944 --> 00:02:51.800
Rich Sutton tied it on
in The Bitter Lesson.

32
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.455
Six days later, Rodney Brooks
replied, "I think Sutton is wrong".

33
00:03:00.033 --> 00:03:03.434
Some kept writing weights
anyway, as proofs and test beds.

34
00:03:03.984 --> 00:03:06.398
Eighty-three years after the oldest tag,

35
00:03:06.398 --> 00:03:10.148
someone has written a network’s
weights by hand, to run a desktop.

36
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.238
The third of June, twenty
twenty-six: Cheng Lou,

37
00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.220
Handwired's author,
makes his first commit.

38
00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.997
Four months and two
renames later: Handwired.

39
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:34.002
Its tutorial: “A numeric program is a fixed
list of operations over 32-bit floats”.

40
00:03:34.452 --> 00:03:39.594
You don't write the list. Builder code,
ordinary JavaScript run once, appends to it.

41
00:03:42.183 --> 00:03:45.445
One guess falls at once,
for the library itself.

42
00:03:45.445 --> 00:03:50.832
The docs: “No tool converts existing
JavaScript source into a program.”

43
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:58.149
Every number the program reads or keeps is
a cell in one table. A toggle has twenty.

44
00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:01.478
A wire is a position:
where a number lives.

45
00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:08.481
Four instructions: a weighted
sum, a gated product,

46
00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:11.580
a snap to a whole number,
a read from a list.

47
00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:18.009
Each step rounds to a thirty-two-bit float,
a number that keeps about seven digits.

48
00:04:18.009 --> 00:04:21.573
Even whole numbers stay exact
only to about seventeen million.

49
00:04:23.058 --> 00:04:26.634
Somebody trained it? The desktop’s README:

50
00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:30.151
“Every weight of the program is written
by builder code, and none is trained.”

51
00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:32.314
That guess was ours.

52
00:04:32.864 --> 00:04:38.204
And the Notes window? “The program
holds no text.” Hang that on the line.

53
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.213
Back to four and a half. In
JavaScript, only the chosen side runs.

54
00:04:49.663 --> 00:04:51.078
A network has no if.

55
00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:58.175
Bend a hinge: flat, then
up. A ramp is all a ReLU is.

56
00:04:58.725 --> 00:05:07.327
This one wakes at four. Three gives nought.
Five gives one. A hundred gives ninety-six.

57
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.134
A second ramp wakes at five.
Take it from the first.

58
00:05:13.684 --> 00:05:17.620
Nought up to four, one from five: a bit.

59
00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.112
Whole numbers sit on the corners: the
whole trick, and the whole caveat.

60
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.838
Two belts carry both answers,
every time: ten and twelve.

61
00:05:27.838 --> 00:05:30.333
The bit opens one
shutter, shuts the other.

62
00:05:32.683 --> 00:05:38.999
Say the side you didn’t choose overflows,
past the biggest float, to infinity.

63
00:05:38.999 --> 00:05:43.038
Nought times infinity isn’t
nought. It isn’t a number at all.

64
00:05:44.517 --> 00:05:50.275
Now four and a half. The bit reads
a half: half of ten, half of twelve.

65
00:05:50.825 --> 00:05:51.474
Eleven.

66
00:05:52.924 --> 00:05:59.588
Every if here is a dimmer switch. The
program’s job: keep it fully on, or fully off.

67
00:06:01.058 --> 00:06:04.761
Past nearly seventeen million,
floats skip whole numbers.

68
00:06:05.311 --> 00:06:08.724
Just past it, the second
ramp’s input rounds down.

69
00:06:08.724 --> 00:06:13.228
A bit meant for nought or
one reads two. Answer: eight.

70
00:06:14.683 --> 00:06:17.330
Another way to make a
bit, in one instruction:

71
00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:21.416
add a huge odd number to a
score, then take it away.

72
00:06:21.966 --> 00:06:26.482
Up there floats sit one apart,
so the score snaps whole.

73
00:06:26.482 --> 00:06:30.986
Ties go to the even side:
a half snaps up, to one.

74
00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:36.167
Each trick is exact. Against what?

75
00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:41.576
The thirty-two-bit calculation, not
JavaScript’s usual sixty-four-bit doubles.

76
00:06:42.126 --> 00:06:46.445
Fit columns in a window seventeen
hundred and thirty-two pixels wide.

77
00:06:46.445 --> 00:06:51.321
Doubles fit seven; thirty-two-bit
floats fall a hair short.

78
00:06:52.808 --> 00:06:57.069
Why not doubles everywhere?
A September study tried.

79
00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:01.364
Of twenty thousand percent labels,
nearly nineteen thousand went wrong.

80
00:07:01.914 --> 00:07:03.807
More precision broke it.

81
00:07:03.807 --> 00:07:09.007
Keep thirty-two-bit order at the rounding
points alone, and every label came back.

82
00:07:11.267 --> 00:07:14.796
The real library won’t compare
until you promise a range.

83
00:07:15.346 --> 00:07:20.802
Promise more, that a is a whole number,
and the comparison takes two operations.

84
00:07:23.267 --> 00:07:28.595
Now break the promise: four and
a half, down a wire tagged whole.

85
00:07:29.145 --> 00:07:32.220
Eleven again. A validator objects.

86
00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:37.970
As the docs put it, “Declarations are
promises from the caller, not proofs.”

87
00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.687
Who tied it on? The caller.
Who doubted it? The validator.

88
00:07:43.687 --> 00:07:46.960
How long it held? While
the input stayed honest.

89
00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:53.170
Promise only a range, and it must
tell the tiniest difference from none.

90
00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:56.222
It magnifies by two to the
hundred and forty-ninth.

91
00:07:56.772 --> 00:08:00.278
The faintest difference
becomes a full bar.

92
00:08:00.278 --> 00:08:05.037
A sixteen-operation comparison, and
four and a half gives ten. Correct.

93
00:08:06.517 --> 00:08:11.520
Sixteen against two. One honest sentence
about your input saves fourteen operations.

94
00:08:19.142 --> 00:08:24.308
Now feed the program's operations into a
loom, a hundred and thirty layers deep.

95
00:08:24.608 --> 00:08:29.518
Each step drops into the first
layer after the steps it needs.

96
00:08:29.868 --> 00:08:35.846
Copper threads add. Indigo threads
subtract. Most of it is empty air.

97
00:08:37.208 --> 00:08:43.013
The README calls it "a giant
matmul": a matrix multiplication,

98
00:08:43.013 --> 00:08:45.555
the sums graphics cards are built for.

99
00:08:45.805 --> 00:08:50.043
Sums of sums are still
sums, so some fold together.

100
00:08:50.043 --> 00:08:53.885
But a gate is a bend:
nothing merges across one.

101
00:08:54.235 --> 00:08:57.775
The longest chain of gates
runs seventy-one deep.

102
00:08:59.167 --> 00:09:04.948
So it is a network, in the plain sense:
weighted sums, with ReLU gates between.

103
00:09:05.098 --> 00:09:06.653
That guess was ours too.

104
00:09:08.142 --> 00:09:14.399
Step back. The wallpaper draws these
same threads: one arc per weight.

105
00:09:17.558 --> 00:09:23.560
And the Notes window? On the thirteenth
of October its text moved to the browser,

106
00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:25.777
and the loom shed most of its threads.

107
00:09:29.542 --> 00:09:35.045
The desktop runs as JavaScript on the
main processor. On a graphics card, an

108
00:09:35.045 --> 00:09:40.304
older build ran fifteen to twenty-seven
times slower, its steps one after another.

109
00:09:40.854 --> 00:09:46.240
So what is a graphics card, a
GPU, for? Hang that on the line.

110
00:09:52.125 --> 00:09:57.570
In the docs' words: "The program runs every
operation on every frame and can't stop

111
00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:03.467
early, so a loop becomes a count, a tree,
a table, or the same work on every item."

112
00:10:04.933 --> 00:10:10.110
The looping lives outside, in the
host: the plain JavaScript around it.

113
00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.869
Flick the toggle, and the
event is queued with its time.

114
00:10:14.169 --> 00:10:22.372
Inputs in. The loom runs once. Next
state copied back. The page moves.

115
00:10:22.672 --> 00:10:27.501
When nothing moves, the clock rests:
between frames, never inside one.

116
00:10:28.975 --> 00:10:32.132
The windows move on springs,
and springs need time.

117
00:10:32.432 --> 00:10:36.228
One step multiplies two
numbers, the window's distance

118
00:10:36.228 --> 00:10:39.629
from home and its speed: at
rest, it comes out moving.

119
00:10:40.179 --> 00:10:45.148
Three hundred steps is that
multiplication, squared and squared again.

120
00:10:45.148 --> 00:10:48.782
JavaScript works it out
ahead, as four numbers;

121
00:10:48.782 --> 00:10:52.113
the program applies them in
fourteen operations, for any count.

122
00:10:55.475 --> 00:11:00.048
Past three hundred steps in one frame,
stepping stops, and every window snaps home.

123
00:11:03.308 --> 00:11:06.013
Replay one session at two frame rates,

124
00:11:06.013 --> 00:11:10.261
and the familiar loop drifts up to three
hundred and fifty-nine pixels apart.

125
00:11:10.561 --> 00:11:15.135
Handwired saves only at
events and fixed checkpoints.

126
00:11:15.135 --> 00:11:21.068
Other frames replay from the last
save, are drawn, and thrown away.

127
00:11:21.068 --> 00:11:25.096
In its tests, every frame
rate then matches exactly.

128
00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:30.861
One run per frame? That
held until mid-September.

129
00:11:30.861 --> 00:11:33.402
Now a busy drag frame
runs about seventeen.

130
00:11:34.892 --> 00:11:40.790
A count. Which row is under the pointer?
A loop stops at the first match.

131
00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:45.257
Here every row answers, and
we count the yeses: three.

132
00:11:48.142 --> 00:11:54.632
A tree. Running totals in a row: each
waits for the last, seven steps deep.

133
00:11:54.632 --> 00:11:58.381
In pairs, then pairs of
pairs: three or four.

134
00:12:02.933 --> 00:12:07.443
In thirty-two-bit floats,
grouping changes the final digit.

135
00:12:07.443 --> 00:12:10.368
The tree is slower here,
but drifts far less.

136
00:12:13.558 --> 00:12:19.595
A table. As arithmetic, you test all
ten thousand rows, zero out all but one,

137
00:12:19.595 --> 00:12:24.066
and add: thirty thousand
operations. Or one index read.

138
00:12:26.517 --> 00:12:29.803
To sort, do the same work on every item:

139
00:12:29.803 --> 00:12:32.994
each card counts who comes
before it. Nothing swaps.

140
00:12:34.683 --> 00:12:40.533
Filtering too: each item counts its place,
and a fixed rack takes the first three.

141
00:12:40.833 --> 00:12:46.045
The desktop's Monitor window admits
it: "Only 64 rows are drawn at a time."

142
00:12:47.517 --> 00:12:53.228
Kleene's finite machine, with everything
unbounded coming from outside? Here it is.

143
00:12:53.778 --> 00:12:59.664
The guide to the project's lab of experiments
says: “One frame is one evaluation

144
00:12:59.664 --> 00:13:06.026
of a fixed-depth circuit”. The host
turns the crank. It never stops early.

145
00:13:11.917 --> 00:13:16.908
Every weight so far was written by
hand. The README's first funny use-case:

146
00:13:17.158 --> 00:13:23.694
“unfreezing the weights and train them
(what does that even do? Let's find out!)”

147
00:13:25.183 --> 00:13:30.953
Take one gated product: a valve.
Along its channel flows an input,

148
00:13:30.953 --> 00:13:37.628
three, times a weight, w. The opening
is g plus b, never below nought.

149
00:13:38.078 --> 00:13:42.571
With w a half, g a
quarter, b minus a tenth:

150
00:13:42.571 --> 00:13:48.109
it opens nought point one five, and
out comes nought point two two five.

151
00:13:48.659 --> 00:13:52.850
Learning needs each
number's slope, or gradient:

152
00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.647
nudge it, and how far
does the output move?

153
00:13:56.197 --> 00:14:02.269
Backwards, w's slope is three times
the opening: nought point four five.

154
00:14:02.269 --> 00:14:05.647
g's is the channel itself: one and a half.

155
00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:13.380
Lower b to minus four tenths. The valve
shuts, and every slope behind it goes dark.

156
00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:19.056
A README in the project
warns: “Without any decay,

157
00:14:19.056 --> 00:14:23.757
a unit whose gate has closed
gets no gradient and stays shut”.

158
00:14:24.307 --> 00:14:29.739
The neurons of 1943 couldn't
learn. These can, if you let them.

159
00:14:31.225 --> 00:14:34.824
Almost every operation runs
backwards by a rule like that:

160
00:14:34.824 --> 00:14:37.528
on the real arithmetic, not the rounding.

161
00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:45.755
The README calls it fully
differentiable: a slope everywhere.

162
00:14:45.755 --> 00:14:47.600
The project itself has doubts.

163
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:53.483
Ask the ramps which way to nudge the input.
At almost every whole number: nowhere.

164
00:14:54.033 --> 00:14:59.350
Differentiable as real arithmetic.
But a decision has no slope to follow.

165
00:15:00.808 --> 00:15:04.349
Only one demo learns: the learned gallery.

166
00:15:04.349 --> 00:15:08.667
From random weights, it learns to place
the photos on rectangles you sketch.

167
00:15:10.933 --> 00:15:16.007
Before each step, plain JavaScript
pairs each photo with a rectangle.

168
00:15:16.007 --> 00:15:19.999
The network learns where.
JavaScript decides who.

169
00:15:21.558 --> 00:15:26.073
Each photo's edges pull toward its
rectangle, but every pull is capped.

170
00:15:26.623 --> 00:15:29.014
Far-off photos vote. They don't shout.

171
00:15:33.792 --> 00:15:38.168
Six hundred steps a second, through
the program's own backward pass.

172
00:15:41.892 --> 00:15:45.758
An early fix snapped the sketch
to a grid, in ordinary code.

173
00:15:45.758 --> 00:15:50.517
Cheng turned it down: “the spirit of
the gallery is to learn something.”

174
00:15:52.767 --> 00:15:58.548
To be fair, that's a small generic network,
not a hand-written program unfrozen.

175
00:15:58.898 --> 00:16:03.623
The lab did unfreeze one app's five
design numbers to follow a drag.

176
00:16:03.623 --> 00:16:08.010
One gradient step solved nearly
two in three moving frames.

177
00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:14.318
A frozen program trained end to end inside
a text-writing network, like nanoGPT?

178
00:16:14.318 --> 00:16:18.021
Not attempted. It hangs as a question.

179
00:16:24.183 --> 00:16:26.713
Six commits in July. In August, none.

180
00:16:27.263 --> 00:16:30.364
The record doesn't say
why. So we won't guess.

181
00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:36.366
July’s lab briefs listed established
conclusions, to take as given.

182
00:16:36.366 --> 00:16:39.175
One: compiling ordinary
code was a dead end.

183
00:16:39.725 --> 00:16:43.602
Who tied it on? A conversation
whose people go unnamed.

184
00:16:46.392 --> 00:16:52.325
On the third of September, an AI agent’s
review retired ten earlier claims, that

185
00:16:52.325 --> 00:16:59.301
one among them. Its narrower replacement:
a defined numeric subset can be compiled.

186
00:16:59.851 --> 00:17:01.278
It held about eight weeks.

187
00:17:03.558 --> 00:17:07.970
One test, learning two additions,
scored nought out of twenty.

188
00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:15.214
Multiply its error by a million, which
should change nothing: twenty out of twenty.

189
00:17:15.214 --> 00:17:18.685
A tiny safety constant
was swamping the slopes.

190
00:17:22.142 --> 00:17:27.401
In July, a checker for the lab's
compiler reported zero mismatches.

191
00:17:27.401 --> 00:17:31.325
The compiled code skipped an early
return, and so did the checker.

192
00:17:31.875 --> 00:17:35.579
As the docs warn, “That the two
evaluators agree doesn't show

193
00:17:35.579 --> 00:17:39.049
that a program does what the
JavaScript it replaces did.”

194
00:17:50.308 --> 00:17:54.580
Every commit is Cheng Lou’s; most also
credit a coding agent, Claude Code.

195
00:17:55.030 --> 00:18:01.716
The review also names an earlier OpenAI agent,
Codex. This film was written with Claude.

196
00:18:02.266 --> 00:18:06.932
Mistakes and catches alike came from
one process, under his direction.

197
00:18:08.592 --> 00:18:10.867
In mid-September, he
settled three questions.

198
00:18:17.125 --> 00:18:20.595
Then October: six hundred
and eighty-nine commits.

199
00:18:21.145 --> 00:18:23.524
Nearly all of them in four days.

200
00:18:26.475 --> 00:18:30.028
Cheng asked whether search
on the GPU was “clearly

201
00:18:30.028 --> 00:18:32.918
more performant”, and if so, worth a demo.

202
00:18:35.183 --> 00:18:38.736
A hundred thousand JSON
documents, small text records,

203
00:18:38.736 --> 00:18:42.496
one per GPU lane, all
running one small step.

204
00:18:43.046 --> 00:18:48.537
So what is a GPU for? The demo's
answer: “It is for many documents”.

205
00:18:51.808 --> 00:18:57.148
On the thirteenth, a hedged write-up
tied on a tag: the GPU is faster.

206
00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:05.382
Search patterns are what Kleene,
back in 1951, called regular events.

207
00:19:05.932 --> 00:19:11.295
As the code says, a new pattern
“writes cells and changes no program.”

208
00:19:12.767 --> 00:19:17.259
A program can't go back, so
search walks each document twice.

209
00:19:17.609 --> 00:19:23.424
From the end, then the start: a longest match
is only known after reading past its end.

210
00:19:25.683 --> 00:19:30.095
The browser's pattern-matcher,
RegExp, on one core:

211
00:19:30.095 --> 00:19:35.388
eight to twenty-eight milliseconds a
keystroke. The search: eleven to fifteen.

212
00:19:35.938 --> 00:19:40.815
One pattern makes it try every
way back: over two seconds.

213
00:19:40.815 --> 00:19:44.181
The program never goes
back: eleven milliseconds.

214
00:19:46.433 --> 00:19:51.111
The same day, a review found
RegExp had one of eighteen cores.

215
00:19:51.461 --> 00:19:58.113
With eight workers, RegExp keeps up, and wins
after a pause, except on that one pattern.

216
00:19:58.663 --> 00:20:00.334
That tag held for hours.

217
00:20:00.884 --> 00:20:05.074
The lab, with good
grace: “RegExp is fast.”

218
00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:12.398
On the fourteenth, Cheng gave the
go-ahead: “Do it quick plz, then publish.”

219
00:20:12.948 --> 00:20:18.508
That evening it went public: “My dear friends,
I’d like to show you my latest toy project”.

220
00:20:22.475 --> 00:20:27.653
On Hacker News, one commenter said it’s
really “write your logic in a DSL that

221
00:20:27.653 --> 00:20:34.003
creates a neural-net-shaped circuit”.
A DSL: a little language for one job.

222
00:20:34.453 --> 00:20:41.522
Another saw a compiler. The docs side with
the first: no tool converts your code.

223
00:20:42.975 --> 00:20:47.781
Nine days after launch, a stranger
tried four and a half: “you get 11.”

224
00:20:48.331 --> 00:20:53.660
Turn our whole-number tag over: the landing
page's hidden comment already said so.

225
00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:57.049
A promise that states its limits is kept.

226
00:20:58.517 --> 00:21:01.535
Another asked what the
website never said: “why?”

227
00:21:02.085 --> 00:21:05.509
That's our blank tag.
We'll keep it for the end.

228
00:21:07.375 --> 00:21:12.715
About the lab, we found no public word: the
strangers' pencils were gentler than its own.

229
00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:22.457
One last experiment. The search demo's JSON
highlighter stops at the first mistake.

230
00:21:22.807 --> 00:21:27.265
Its parser is a table, and valid
input never reads most of it.

231
00:21:29.917 --> 00:21:37.293
Train only the unread entries,
to keep going after a mistake,

232
00:21:37.293 --> 00:21:40.415
and valid JSON parses exactly as before.

233
00:21:42.892 --> 00:21:48.639
Before testing, the lab tied on a
tag: training can fill those entries.

234
00:21:48.639 --> 00:21:53.038
Then it fixed its rule for failure,
and committed the test sets first.

235
00:21:55.225 --> 00:21:57.604
JSON: “a clear success.”

236
00:21:58.854 --> 00:22:00.874
Though on new kinds of mistake,

237
00:22:00.874 --> 00:22:05.947
and on whole payloads written by
agents, hand-written rules still won.

238
00:22:08.225 --> 00:22:12.868
For TOML, another format: “By the rule
fixed beforehand the track failed”.

239
00:22:13.218 --> 00:22:16.398
It lost to a one-line
hand-written fallback, every run.

240
00:22:16.698 --> 00:22:22.560
The README is careful: the failure “does
not say that training cannot work there.”

241
00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:27.702
This is how a belief should end:
by a rule written before the test.

242
00:22:30.375 --> 00:22:35.773
The third parser's final test hasn't
run yet. That question stays open.

243
00:22:36.223 --> 00:22:38.289
So does the lab's own list.

244
00:22:38.739 --> 00:22:42.919
It says plainly: “a general
JavaScript compiler”

245
00:22:42.919 --> 00:22:45.542
is “not delivered by these experiments.”

246
00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:53.803
Every belief in the film
hangs over the time it held.

247
00:22:56.258 --> 00:23:01.702
Now re-hang them the way the loom does:
each belief one layer after those it needs.

248
00:23:02.252 --> 00:23:04.318
The film's teaching order, not history.

249
00:23:09.058 --> 00:23:12.784
Send one wave of light
through it. Every tag lights.

250
00:23:13.834 --> 00:23:17.304
The struck ones ran too.
Multiplied by nought.

251
00:23:18.767 --> 00:23:24.154
Three beliefs are still argued over.
Learn the weights, don't write them.

252
00:23:24.154 --> 00:23:29.401
Fully differentiable. And the README's
claim to take “regular JavaScript”.

253
00:23:29.951 --> 00:23:36.742
To the stranger's “why?”, the README's nearest
answers: “for fun”, and “Let's find out!”

254
00:23:38.225 --> 00:23:41.452
The notes window ends: “Wire
one more neuron by hand”.

255
00:23:43.725 --> 00:23:48.356
Who ties the next one on? Who
doubts it? How long will it hold?

256
00:23:49.506 --> 00:23:52.106
It's waiting for the next pair of hands.
